  "tier": "told-once",
  "plain": "The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have finally got a positive result from his anti-gravity experiments in Paris, and when he flew back to the US he was picked up at the airport by a man named Robert Sarbacher in his big Cadillac. Michels tells this himself, once to the writer Whitley Strieber and once in his own documentary on Brown, drawing on Brown's family and biographer, Linda Brown and Paul Schatzkin. In the documentary he dates the trip to 1956 and says the engineer Henri Coanda and a man named Jacques Cornillon brought Brown to France. The point of the story is the pickup: it would tie Brown's anti-gravity work directly to Sarbacher."
}
